artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider hierarchical planning models in which decisions are made at multiple levels (e.g., strategic, tactical, operational), and where higher-level models work with aggregated entities — such as aggregated products, aggregated land units (stands), or coarsened spatial resolution — while lower-level models must disaggregate the higher-level decisions back to the original fine-grained entities to produce implementable plans.

For purely linear-programming (LP) formulations, classical aggregation theory provides conditions under which an aggregate problem yields a solution whose disaggregation is feasible and optimal (or bounded in suboptimality) for the original disaggregated LP. These results rely heavily on convexity and on the structure of LP optimal bases, and they do not extend directly when the underlying models include integer or binary decision variables (for example, variables representing whether to build a road segment, whether to harvest a particular spatial unit, or adjacency/contiguity constraints among discrete units).

Formally: let the disaggregated problem be a mixed-integer program (MIP)
   min { c^T x + d^T y : A x + B y ≤ b, x ∈ R^n_+, y ∈ Z^p_+ },
and let an aggregation be specified by linear maps x = P x̄, y = Q ȳ (with appropriate integrality on ȳ) yielding an aggregate MIP over (x̄, ȳ). Develop systematic aggregation and disaggregation procedures for such hierarchical MIP planning models that:
 (i) define meaningful aggregations of products, units, and/or spatial resolution that preserve integrality structure;
 (ii) provide a disaggregation operator that maps any optimal (or near-optimal) aggregate solution to a feasible solution of the disaggregated MIP;
 (iii) yield provable bounds on the optimality gap between the disaggregated solution and the true optimum of the original MIP;
 (iv) accommodate spatial/combinatorial features such as road-network build decisions and adjacency or contiguity constraints among discrete spatial units.

Provide such a framework, or prove obstructions showing that no such framework with the desired guarantees can exist in full generality.
